<h2>Drama and the Human Condition</h2>
<p><em>How Hamlet's language reveals his state of mind</em></p>
<p>One of the most famous soliloquies in all of theater is Hamlet's "To be, or not to be" speech from Act 3, Scene 1 of Shakespeare's play. Watch one or more of the performances in the Watch &amp; Listen section of this unit's lesson (or find another version you prefer). This unit introduces the <strong>soliloquy</strong> as a dramatic device that gives the audience access to a character's inner thoughts. In this speech, Hamlet weighs the value of living against the release of death.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>The prompt</strong></p>
<p>Using the vocabulary from this unit (<strong>diction</strong>, <strong>thought</strong>, and <strong>character</strong>), analyze how the language of this soliloquy reveals Hamlet's state of mind. Pay attention to specific word choices and the structure of his argument. What makes this speech <em>dramatic</em> rather than merely philosophical?</p>
</div></div>
<p>A good response quotes a few specific phrases and says what each does. Consider whether Hamlet reaches a conclusion, whether he seems to be convincing himself or another person, and how different the speech would feel if another character were listening.</p>
